                    </tr>
                    <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-800/50 transition-colors duration-150">
                      <td class="p-4 font-medium border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">4. Your Public Profile</td>
                      <td class="p-4 text-center"><a href="#profile" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">Jump →</a></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-800/50 transition-colors duration-150">
                      <td class="p-4 font-medium border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">5. Network &amp; Connections</td>
                      <td class="p-4 text-center"><a href="#network" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">Jump →</a></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-800/50 transition-colors duration-150">
                      <td class="p-4 font-medium border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">6. Points &amp; The Leaderboard</td>
                      <td class="p-4 text-center"><a href="#leaderboard" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">Jump →</a></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-800/50 transition-colors duration-150">
                      <td class="p-4 font-medium border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">7. Top Contributors</td>
                      <td class="p-4 text-center"><a href="#contributors" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">Jump →</a></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-800/50 transition-colors duration-150">
                      <td class="p-4 font-medium border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">8. Privacy &amp; Domain Modes</td>
                      <td class="p-4 text-center"><a href="#privacy" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">Jump →</a></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-800/50 transition-colors duration-150">
                      <td class="p-4 font-medium border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">9. RSS &amp; Staying Updated</td>
                      <td class="p-4 text-center"><a href="#rss" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">Jump →</a></td>
                    </tr>
                    <tr class="hover:bg-gray-50 dark:hover:bg-gray-800/50 transition-colors duration-150">
                      <td class="p-4 font-medium">10. FAQ</td>
                      <td class="p-4 text-center"><a href="#faq" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">Jump →</a></td>
                    </tr>
                  </tbody>
                </table>
              </div>
              <div class="mt-6 text-center text-sm md:text-base text-gray-600 dark:text-gray-400 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4 md:gap-6">
                <a href="#how-to-post" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">Post Your First Torch</a>
                <a href="#faq" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">Frequently Asked Questions</a>
              </div>
            </nav>

            <!-- CTA Box -->
            <div class="mt-12 p-4 md:p-6 rounded-2xl border border-orange-200 dark:border-orange-800 bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-sm text-center">
              <p class="text-xl md:text-2xl font-black mb-4 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-400 to-pink-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                Ready to share?
              </p>
              <p class="text-lg font-medium text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mb-6 leading-relaxed max-w-2xl mx-auto">
                Run any audit, post it as a torch, and get a permanent shareable link.<br class="hidden sm:block">
                <span class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 font-semibold">Free • Public • Opt-in.</span>
              </p>
              <a href="/community/"
                 class="inline-block px-8 py-5 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500 via-pink-500 to-purple-600 text-white font-bold text-lg rounded-xl hover:brightness-110 hover:scale-[1.03] transition-all duration-300 shadow-xl shadow-orange-500/30">
                See the Community Feed →
              </a>
            </div>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 1 ================= -->
            <section id="what-is-a-torch" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500 to-pink-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                1. What is a Torch?
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  A <strong>torch</strong> is a public audit result. Every audit you run produces a 0–100 score and a per-module breakdown. When you click <strong>Post to Community</strong> after an audit, that result becomes a torch: it gets a page, a share image, and a permanent link.
                </p>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed mt-4">
                  Torches do three things at once:
                </p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li><strong>Gives you a shareable link.</strong> Instead of "I scored 96 on NUSA", you send <code class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400">traffictorch.net/torch/9/</code> and everyone sees the full breakdown.</li>
                  <li><strong>Contributes to the community.</strong> Other SEOs browse the feed at <a href="/community/" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">/community/</a> to see what's working and what isn't.</li>
                  <li><strong>Ranks you on the leaderboard.</strong> If you submit to the leaderboard too, your torch competes with every other audit ever shared.</li>
                </ul>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed mt-6">
                  Nothing is posted automatically. You choose what to share and when. Every torch is opt-in, every field is editable after posting, and every torch can be deleted at any time.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left">
                  <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 2 ================= -->
            <section id="posting" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-purple-500 to-indigo-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                2. Posting Your First Torch
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  You don't need an account to run an audit. You <em>do</em> need one to post a torch, because a torch is tied to your public profile.
                </p>

                <h3 id="how-to-post" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8 scroll-mt-24">The four-step flow</h3>
                <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li>Run any audit on any page. The dashboard is at <a href="/dashboard/" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">/dashboard/</a>, or use any of the standalone tool pages.</li>
                  <li>Wait for the results to come in. Most audits finish in 10–30 seconds.</li>
                  <li>Click <strong>Post to Community</strong> below the score.</li>
                  <li>Fill in the optional note (up to 360 characters) and choose a domain mode. Submit.</li>
                </ol>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">Editing after posting</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  You can edit the note, category, and page title at any time from the feed. Just click <strong>Edit</strong> on your own torch card. Deleting a torch is equally easy — click <strong>Delete</strong> and the torch disappears from the feed, the leaderboard, and any sitemap within seconds.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">One torch per audit, or re-share?</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Each audit produces one torch. If you re-run the audit later and want to share the new score, you can post a new torch. Old torches stay linked to their original score so history isn't rewritten.
                </p>

                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left">
                  <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 3 ================= -->
            <section id="torch-pages" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-blue-500 to-indigo-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                3. Torch Pages
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Every torch has its own page at <code class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400">/torch/:id/</code>. It's the page you send to someone when you say "look at this audit".
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">What's on a torch page</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li><strong>The score</strong>, in a colour-coded circle: green above 80, amber 60–79, red below 60.</li>
                  <li><strong>The domain and page title</strong>, so readers know what was audited.</li>
                  <li><strong>The note</strong> you wrote — up to 360 characters of your own commentary.</li>
                  <li><strong>The module breakdown</strong>, every individual signal and its score, in the same format as the audit itself.</li>
                  <li><strong>The author chip</strong> — a link back to your profile, plus your points.</li>
                  <li><strong>Comments</strong> — the same threading as the community feed.</li>
                  <li><strong>Author tab</strong> — bio, socials, stats, and a link to your full profile.</li>
                  <li><strong>Network tab</strong> — the author's own connections, if they're public.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">What happens after you share</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  When you paste the URL into Slack, Discord, or X, the recipient sees a rich preview: the score, the domain, and the page title on a dark gradient card with Traffic Torch branding. That image is generated on the fly for every torch, so you never have to make one.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">Search engines see the torch too</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Torch pages are fully server-rendered and indexed. They include structured data that tells Google the page is a technical article about the audited URL, authored by you. If a torch gets linked from outside, it can appear in search results on its own — which is a very cheap way to make your audit page findable.
                </p>

                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left">
                  <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 4 ================= -->
            <section id="profile" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500 to-pink-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                4. Your Public Profile
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Every account gets a profile at <code class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400">/torcher/:username/</code>. It's a single page that shows everything you've chosen to make public.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">What shows on your profile</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li><strong>Display name, @username, role</strong> and role badge.</li>
                  <li><strong>Bio</strong> — up to 160 characters.</li>
                  <li><strong>Job title, company, location</strong> — optional, editable.</li>
                  <li><strong>Social links</strong> — one website and up to two socials.</li>
                  <li><strong>Stats</strong> — audits shared, network size, points, months on the site.</li>
                  <li><strong>Three tabs</strong> — Shared Audits, Network, About.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">What you control</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  From the profile edit page you can set:
                </p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li><strong>Avatar</strong> — three presets to choose from.</li>
                  <li><strong>Profile visibility</strong> — public (indexable) or private (404 for strangers).</li>
                  <li><strong>Show or hide your network</strong> — whether your connections list appears on the page.</li>
                  <li><strong>Allow or block network adds</strong> — turn off incoming network requests.</li>
                  <li><strong>Auto-share new audits</strong> — post every completed audit to the feed automatically.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">Why profile SEO matters</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Public profiles are indexed with structured data. If you're an SEO, designer, or developer who publishes audits regularly, your profile becomes a discoverable landing page for your name. Anyone searching "&lt;your name&gt; SEO audit" can find your audit history in one click.
                </p>

                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left">
                  <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 5 ================= -->
            <section id="network" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-emerald-600 to-teal-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                5. Network &amp; Connections
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Your <strong>network</strong> is the list of other Traffic Torch users you've connected with. It's a simple, one-way opt-in system: you add someone, they get a notification, and both of you appear in each other's Network tab.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">How to add someone</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li>On any torch page, click <strong>+ Add to Network</strong> on the author chip.</li>
                  <li>On any profile page, click the same button in the header.</li>
                  <li>Or go to <strong>/dashboard/ → Network → Find People</strong> and search by display name or @username.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">What the network gives you</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li><strong>Faster identification.</strong> In the community feed, posts from your network are flagged with "In Network ✓" so you know whose work you're looking at.</li>
                  <li><strong>Priority filtering.</strong> On your own profile, the Network tab is a one-click shortcut to everyone you follow.</li>
                  <li><strong>Visible social proof.</strong> Your network count appears in your stats alongside your points.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">Removing someone</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Click <strong>In Network ✓</strong> on their torch, or <strong>Remove</strong> on their row in your Network tab. It's instant and doesn't notify them.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">The invite option</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Not on Traffic Torch yet? Use <strong>/dashboard/ → Invite</strong> to send someone a signup link by email. They'll get a friendly email naming you, with a link to your profile and a one-click signup.
                </p>

                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left">
                  <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 6 ================= -->
            <section id="leaderboard" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500 to-pink-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                6. Points &amp; The Leaderboard
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Traffic Torch has two related systems: <strong>points</strong> (per user) and the <strong>leaderboard</strong> (per audit submission). They're separate but complementary.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">How points are earned</h3>
                <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                  <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse min-w-[500px] text-sm">
                    <thead>
                      <tr class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800/80">
                        <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Action</th>
                        <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Points</th>
                      </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                      <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Post a torch</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">+10</td></tr>
                      <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Leave a comment</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">+5</td></tr>
                      <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Submit to the leaderboard</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">+10</td></tr>
                      <tr><td class="p-3">Submit a bug, feature, or docs contribution</td><td class="p-3">+25</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                </div>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed mt-6">
                  Points are additive and permanent. They appear next to your name on every torch, comment, and network row. There's no expiry, no decay, and no cap.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">How the leaderboard works</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  The <a href="/leader-board/" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">/leader-board/</a> page ranks every audit that a user has explicitly submitted. When you click <strong>Submit to Leaderboard</strong> on a completed audit, that score enters the running. Re-running the audit keeps the higher of the two scores.
                </p>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li><strong>Podium</strong> — the top 3 all-time, with medals.</li>
                  <li><strong>Hall of Fame</strong> — the top 12, expandable to more.</li>
                  <li><strong>Filter by tool</strong> — 16 tabs, one per tool, plus "All Tools".</li>
                  <li><strong>Top Contributors</strong> — the community members with the most points, separate from raw audit scores.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">Embedding the #1 badge</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  If your site ever takes the #1 spot, the leaderboard page generates an embeddable badge you can paste on your own site. It auto-updates as your rank changes, and links back to the leaderboard.
                </p>

                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left">
                  <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 7 ================= -->
            <section id="contributors" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-purple-500 to-indigo-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                7. Top Contributors
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Every leaderboard page shows a <strong>Top Contributors</strong> panel alongside the score ranking. It's a different kind of list: instead of who has the best audit, it shows who has given the most back to the community.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">Who ends up on it</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Anyone who has earned at least one point appears. The ranking is by total points, then by contribution count, then by user ID for stability. Contributors who submit a bug report, a feature idea, a docs fix, or a tool enhancement of their own are featured with a bio snippet, role badge, and links to their site and socials.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">Why contribute?</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li><strong>Points</strong> — 25 per contribution, the highest single-action award on the site.</li>
                  <li><strong>Profile promotion</strong> — contributors are showcased publicly with a link back to their profile, and most list their company or site.</li>
                  <li><strong>Direct impact</strong> — every accepted bug, feature, or docs fix ships in the next release. Contributors are credited in the changelog.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">How to submit</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Use the contribution form from any tool page, or the footer's Contact link. Describe what you found or what you want to see, pick a category (bug, feature, feedback, or docs), and submit. Contributions are reviewed manually, but most get a response within a day or two.
                </p>

                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left">
                  <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 8 ================= -->
            <section id="privacy" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-cyan-600 to-blue-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                8. Privacy &amp; Domain Modes
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Not every audit is meant for public eyes. Traffic Torch gives you three domain modes when posting a torch so you can control exactly how much is revealed.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">The three modes</h3>
                <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                  <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse min-w-[500px] text-sm">
                    <thead>
                      <tr class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800/80">
                        <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Mode</th>
                        <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">What shows</th>
                        <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Search indexing</th>
                      </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                      <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><strong>Full</strong></td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Domain and full URL. Anyone can click through to the audited page.</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Indexed</td></tr>
                      <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><strong>Domain</strong></td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Domain only (e.g. <code>example.com</code>). The full path is hidden.</td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Indexed</td></tr>
                      <tr><td class="p-3"><strong>Hidden</strong></td><td class="p-3">Custom label instead of the domain (e.g. "Client A"). URL is masked everywhere on the site.</td><td class="p-3">Noindex (torch still works, but never appears in search)</td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                </div>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">What is never stored</h3>
                <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-2 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                  <li>Your email address is never public.</li>
                  <li>Your IP address is never logged or stored in the audit tables.</li>
                  <li>The HTML of audited pages is never kept after the score is computed.</li>
                  <li>Your location, if you fill it in, is free text you typed — not derived from IP.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">Deleting a torch</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Click <strong>Delete</strong> on any torch you posted. It disappears from the feed, the leaderboard, all sitemaps, and any OG image within seconds. The URL 404s. Nothing is soft-deleted for more than the cache TTL.
                </p>

                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left">
                  <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 9 ================= -->
            <section id="rss" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center md:text-left text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-purple-500 to-indigo-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                9. RSS &amp; Staying Updated
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Every public stream on Traffic Torch has an RSS feed. Subscribe in your reader of choice and you'll see new torches as they're posted without touching the site.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">The three feeds</h3>
                <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto mt-4">
                  <table class="w-full text-left border-collapse min-w-[500px] text-sm">
                    <thead>
                      <tr class="bg-gray-100 dark:bg-gray-800/80">
                        <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">Feed</th>
                        <th class="p-3 font-semibold text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700">URL</th>
                      </tr>
                    </thead>
                    <tbody class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200">
                      <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><strong>All torches</strong></td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><a href="/torches/feed.xml" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">/torches/feed.xml</a></td></tr>
                      <tr><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><strong>Per user</strong></td><td class="p-3 border-b border-gray-200 dark:border-gray-700"><a href="/torcher/traffictorch/feed.xml" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">/torcher/:username/feed.xml</a></td></tr>
                      <tr><td class="p-3"><strong>Per tool</strong></td><td class="p-3"><a href="/tools/nusa-tool/torches/feed.xml" class="text-orange-600 dark:text-orange-400 hover:underline">/tools/:tool/torches/feed.xml</a></td></tr>
                    </tbody>
                  </table>
                </div>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">What's in the feed</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Each item contains the torch title, the audited domain, the score, the note, and a link back to the torch page. Feeds update within seconds of a new torch being posted, and every feed entry is a permanent link.
                </p>

                <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 mt-8">Autodiscovery</h3>
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Every profile page and every tool feed page has an autodiscovery tag in its HTML head, so most feed readers will find the right URL automatically when you paste in the page's web address.
                </p>

                <p class="mt-8 text-center md:text-left">
                  <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= SECTION 10 ================= -->
            <section id="faq" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black mt-16 mb-12 text-center bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-400 to-pink-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                10. Frequently Asked Questions
              </h2>
              <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto space-y-4">

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300">
                  <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 dark:hover:text-orange-400 transition">
                    What is a torch on Traffic Torch?
                    <span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300">▼</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed prose prose-orange dark:prose-invert">
                    <p>A torch is a public audit result you choose to share. Every tool run produces a 0–100 score and a per-module breakdown. When you click <strong>Post to Community</strong>, that audit becomes a torch with its own page at <code>/torch/:id/</code>, a share image, and a permanent link you can send to anyone.</p>
                  </div>
                </details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300">
                  <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 dark:hover:text-orange-400 transition">
                    Do I need an account to view the community?
                    <span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300">▼</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed prose prose-orange dark:prose-invert">
                    <p>No. The community feed at <a href="/community/">/community/</a>, the leaderboard at <a href="/leader-board/">/leader-board/</a>, every profile at <code>/torcher/:username/</code>, and every torch page at <code>/torch/:id/</code> are all fully public. You only need an account to post a torch, leave a comment, or add someone to your network.</p>
                  </div>
                </details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300">
                  <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 dark:hover:text-orange-400 transition">
                    How do I earn points on Traffic Torch?
                    <span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300">▼</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed prose prose-orange dark:prose-invert">
                    <p>Points come from four actions: posting a torch (+10), leaving a comment (+5), joining the leaderboard with a shared audit (+10), and submitting a bug report, feature idea, or docs contribution (+25). Points are additive and permanent. They appear next to your name everywhere on the site and rank you on the leaderboard's top contributors section.</p>
                  </div>
                </details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300">
                  <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 dark:hover:text-orange-400 transition">
                    How does the leaderboard work?
                    <span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300">▼</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed prose prose-orange dark:prose-invert">
                    <p>The leaderboard at <a href="/leader-board/">/leader-board/</a> ranks every audit that a user has explicitly submitted. You can filter by tool, view the top 3 podium, browse the top 12 hall of fame, and see the top contributors by points earned. When a URL is re-audited, the higher of the two scores is kept.</p>
                  </div>
                </details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300">
                  <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 dark:hover:text-orange-400 transition">
                    Can I hide my domain from the torch or community?
                    <span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300">▼</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed prose prose-orange dark:prose-invert">
                    <p>Yes. When you post a torch you can choose to hide the domain, show it as-is, or show only the domain without the full path. Hidden torches still get a <code>/torch/:id/</code> page and are still shareable, but they are marked noindex and the domain is masked on the page itself.</p>
                  </div>
                </details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300">
                  <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 dark:hover:text-orange-400 transition">
                    How do I connect with other SEOs?
                    <span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300">▼</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed prose prose-orange dark:prose-invert">
                    <p>Click <strong>+ Add to Network</strong> on any torch page, profile page, or comment. Both users' profiles then appear in each other's Network list, and you both get a notification. You can search for users by name or username at <strong>/dashboard/ → Network → Find People</strong>.</p>
                  </div>
                </details>

                <details class="group bg-white/5 dark:bg-black/20 backdrop-blur-xl rounded-2xl border border-white/10 overflow-hidden transition-all duration-300">
                  <summary class="flex justify-between items-center cursor-pointer p-4 text-xl font-semibold text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 hover:text-orange-500 dark:hover:text-orange-400 transition">
                    Is there an RSS feed?
                    <span class="text-2xl text-orange-500 group-open:rotate-180 transition-transform duration-300">▼</span>
                  </summary>
                  <div class="px-2 pb-6 pt-2 text-gray-700 dark:text-gray-300 leading-relaxed prose prose-orange dark:prose-invert">
                    <p>Yes. There are three: a global feed of all torches at <a href="/torches/feed.xml">/torches/feed.xml</a>, a per-user feed at <code>/torcher/:username/feed.xml</code>, and a per-tool feed at <code>/tools/:tool/torches/feed.xml</code>. Subscribe in any feed reader to get new torches as they're posted.</p>
                  </div>
                </details>

              </div>
              <p class="mt-6 text-center md:text-left text-sm">
                <a href="#table-of-contents" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline font-medium">← Return to TOC</a>
              </p>
            </section>

            <!-- ================= CONCLUSION ================= -->
            <section id="conclusion" class="mb-20 scroll-mt-24">
              <h2 class="text-4xl md:text-4xl font-black text-center text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-100 mb-10 bg-gradient-to-r from-orange-500 via-pink-600 to-purple-600 bg-clip-text text-transparent">
                Join In
              </h2>
              <div class="prose prose-lg prose-orange dark:prose-invert max-w-none text-center md:text-left">
                <p class="text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  The community is what makes individual audit results useful. One torch is a data point. Hundreds of torches become a live map of what's working across the web right now — and every one was posted by someone who chose to share.
                </p>
                <p class="mt-4 text-gray-800 dark:text-gray-200 leading-relaxed">
                  Post your first torch. Add three people to your network. Comment on a torch that surprises you. The whole system gets better with each contribution.
                </p>
              </div>
